00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.010
I dunno, that's gonna be a weird time

00:33:36.010 --> 00:33:37.283
if we get to that point,

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:40.250
with the evolution of technology.

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:41.910
But you kinda fast forwarded beyond that,

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:43.880
which is an even more interesting place,

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:46.610
which is not only is it being stored

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:48.030
on the cloud-based system,

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:51.030
but the interface between your brain

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:53.460
and the cloud-based system is so seamless

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:56.430
that going back into the memory feels like

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:58.270
just reliving the memory.

00:33:58.270 --> 00:34:01.810
So every sensation, not
just the sight and sound,

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:04.920
but also the feel and your
emotions and everything.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:08.840
Now there's also a book, I
think it was rec in effect

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:10.050
way back, by Blake Crouch,

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:12.817
one of my favorite writers, "Recursion",

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:17.340
where he plays around with
this concept of memory,

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:19.610
and being able to enter
back into a memory.

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:22.330
And basically the underlying
philosophy of the book

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:27.330
is that the only thing we
really consist of is memory.

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:33.490
Like that's kind of all that
we've got is this timeline

00:34:33.490 --> 00:34:36.930
that we've sort of recorded.

00:34:36.930 --> 00:34:40.610
I don't understand, what about
the present, the nowness.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:42.103
Like you've got now.

00:34:44.820 --> 00:34:46.970
And you've got an
aspiration to the future.

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:49.640
You've actually got a lotta things.

00:34:49.640 --> 00:34:50.473
Yeah I know.

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:53.760
I'm just throwin' all
the, there's just a lot

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:54.973
to this argument.

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:59.320
But I guess we can just zoom
back in on the present day,

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:00.490
because we don't have that technology.

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:01.330
In the future I--

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:03.060
In the future will be a moot point.

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:04.290
I think it--

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:06.180
You won't be making that decision.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.640
I think if you could have constant access

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.300
to everything that's happened to you,

00:35:11.300 --> 00:35:16.300
in pristine, probably POV, vantage point,

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:19.560
I don't think it would be
healthy, I just don't think

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:20.490
it would be healthy to have it.

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:23.050
I think it would just, it doesn't,

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:26.713
it seems like it would
work for like, in court.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:29.247
You know it's like--

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:32.000
No but think about it.

00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:34.300
It starts getting wild because,

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:37.230
if we can get to a place where the--

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:38.640
Living in the past is not healthy.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:40.430
The interface, yeah but hold on,

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:42.030
this isn't about living in the past.

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.350
If the interface is seamless,

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:49.120
then if I can have the experience
of going to Joshua Tree,

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:50.857
without even going to Joshua Tree.

00:35:50.857 --> 00:35:52.080
I'm not talkin' about virtual reality,

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:54.380
I'm talking about a literal,

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:57.220
the experience is being
piped into my brain.

00:35:57.220 --> 00:36:00.290
At that point photos, it's just like,

